---
// Getting Started Component for MirDB Homepage
// Provides installation instructions, configuration examples, and usage guide
---

<section id="getting-started" class="py-16 px-4 bg-slate-900">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-8 text-center">Getting Started</h2>

    <!-- Installation -->
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-orange-500 mb-4">1. Installation</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-300 mb-4">Clone the repository and build MirDB using Cargo:</p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-800 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-slate-300 installation"># Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/yetone/mirdb.git
cd mirdb

# Build the project
cargo build --release

# Run MirDB server with config
cargo run --release -- -c etc/mirdb.toml</code></pre>
    </div>

    <!-- Configuration -->
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-orange-500 mb-4">2. Configuration</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-300 mb-4">Create a configuration file in TOML format:</p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-800 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-slate-300 config toml"># etc/mirdb.toml
addr = "0.0.0.0:12333"

max_level = 7
work_dir = "/tmp/mirdb"

sst_max_size = "100M"
mem_table_max_size = "4M"
mem_table_max_height = 32

imm_mem_table_max_count = 16
block_size = "4K"
block_restart_interval = 16

l0_compaction_trigger = 4
thread_sleep_ms = 500</code></pre>
    </div>

    <!-- Connecting -->
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-orange-500 mb-4">3. Connecting with a Memcached Client</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-300 mb-4">MirDB is compatible with any memcached client. Connect using telnet, netcat, or any memcached library:</p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-800 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-slate-300"># Connect using telnet
telnet localhost 12333

# Or using netcat (nc)
nc localhost 12333</code></pre>
    </div>

    <!-- Basic Operations -->
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-orange-500 mb-4">4. Basic Operations</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-300 mb-4">Once connected, use SET, GET, and DELETE commands:</p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-800 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-slate-300"># Set a value (key: mykey, flags: 0, ttl: 0, bytes: 5)
set mykey 0 0 5
hello
STORED

# Get a value
get mykey
VALUE mykey 0 5
hello
END

# Delete a value
delete mykey
DELETED</code></pre>
    </div>

    <!-- Client Library Example -->
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-orange-500 mb-4">5. Using a Client Library</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-300 mb-4">You can use any memcached client library. Here's an example using Python:</p>
      <pre class="bg-slate-800 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-slate-300">import memcache

# Connect to MirDB
client = memcache.Client(['127.0.0.1:12333'])

# Set a value
client.set('mykey', 'hello')

# Get the value
value = client.get('mykey')
print(value)  # Output: hello

# Delete the key
client.delete('mykey')</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
